{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791561622,"nonce":"a03dgWIdqKw0WQaxY/CAsOUxo64=","message":"Checked against the live code. Gate tests are in 8d2b8103 (crates/blinc_cn/tests/theming_precedence.rs).\n\nUnblocked: the ashui work this was queued behind has landed (CJK fallback, taffy 0.14, baselines).\n\nConfirmed:\n- An app stylesheet beats a cn component's own radius and fill (two live tests pass today). That is the contract theming rests on.\n- A fluent call loses to a stylesheet rule: rounded(3) against border-radius 20px resolves 20 (ignored test).\n- The only CSS-to-props writer is RenderTree::apply_element_style_to_props, which overwrites unconditionally. Fluent calls write Div fields (e.g. rounded() sets border_radius and border_radius_explicit); RenderProps holds only the resolved value, so nothing records who wrote it.\n\nCorrection to this issue: removing a rule does NOT currently restore the component default. After set_stylesheet with the rule gone, the button stays at radius 0 instead of returning to 6 (ignored test removing_a_rule_restores_the_components_default). The base_styles snapshot covers state changes such as hover, not rule removal. So the Default tier is also a fix for stale values after a theme switch or hot reload, not only for precedence.\n\nAnswers to the two open questions:\n- Origin: no. Sheets cascade by Stylesheet::merge into one sheet (windowed.rs add_stylesheet), so a rule's source is lost and only order and specificity remain. A Default tier for cn's own CSS needs an origin on each rule, or cn's sheet kept as a separate base layer below the app's.\n- Slots: not decided. Putting a small per-property bitset beside the values on RenderProps keeps the values where every reader already looks.\n\nOrder that changes no behaviour until the last step: (1) record the tier per property, written by the fluent methods and the CSS pass, read by nobody; (2) mark cn's roughly 300 fluent defaults as Default through base() or a scope guard; (3) give the stylesheet pass a lower layer to fall back to, which fixes rule removal; (4) only then let Owned beat the stylesheet, which is the one user-visible behaviour change and should be a single reviewed switch.","files":null}]}